{{if .Pending}}
<section hx-get="{{.URL}}" hx-trigger="load delay:2s" hx-swap="outerHTML">
  <h2>What the strategist did not try</h2>
  <div class="figure"><div class="ins-body">
    <p class="ins-pending"><span class="pulse" aria-hidden="true"></span>Replaying the game at values the doctrines never used…</p>
  </div></div>
</section>
{{else if .Error}}
<section>
  <h2>What the strategist did not try</h2>
  <div class="figure"><div class="ins-body"><p class="ins-caveat">The sweep failed: {{.Error}}</p></div></div>
</section>
{{else if .Sweeps}}
<section>
  <h2>What the strategist did not try</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The sensitivity read above can only speak about values the strategist actually chose. This
    replays the same states with one input overridden and everything else left as that window had
    it, so it can reach values the game never saw. The big number is <b>held states</b> — how often a requirement set was satisfiable, summed over
    rules; below it, how many rules could fire at all.
  </p>
  <div class="figure">
    <div class="figure-head">
      <span class="figure-title">Rules the set could still use, by input value</span>
      <span class="legend"><span style="font-family:var(--mono)">shaded = inside the range the strategist used</span></span>
    </div>
    {{range .Sweeps}}
    <div class="sweep">
      <span class="sweep-knob">{{.Knob}}</span>
      <span class="sweep-verdict{{if not .Material}} quiet{{end}}">{{.Verdict}}</span>
      <div class="sweep-points">
        {{range .Points}}
        <div class="sp{{if .Actual}} used{{end}}">
          <span class="sp-v">{{printf "%.1f" .Value}}</span>
          <span class="sp-n">{{.Held}}</span>
          <span class="sp-l">{{.Live}} rules</span>
        </div>
        {{end}}
      </div>
      <span class="sweep-meta">used {{printf "%.2f" .UsedLow}}–{{printf "%.2f" .UsedHigh}} · held {{.Worst.Held}}–{{.Best.Held}} · live rules {{.Worst.Live}}–{{.Best.Live}}</span>
    </div>
    {{end}}
  </div>
  <p class="lede">
    These states came from the game that happened, so a sweep says whether a rule would have been
    <i>satisfiable</i> in the situations that arose — not that the game would have gone
    differently. Different rules produce different situations from the first tick.
  </p>
</section>
{{end}}
